{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And Judah said, Bring her forth, and let her be burnt</b> — הוציאוה ותשרף is the notarikon of שם בן ארבע (Shem son of four). Teaches that HaShem prepared for her the signs that had been lost from her." "<b>Bring her forth, and let her be burnt</b> Rashi explained: because she was the daughter of Shem, and a daughter of a priest who commits harlotry is punished by burning. Ramban objected: that which is written, 'a daughter of a priest who commits harlotry, by burning' — that refers to a betrothed or married woman, but a yevama awaiting levirate marriage, even if a priestess, is only a negative prohibition. And if you say, because levirate marriage applied among the children of Noah and their warning is their death-penalty — this is refuted in Sanhedrin that levirate marriage does not apply to the children of Noah at all. And furthermore, every death-penalty stated for the children of Noah is only by the sword. And furthermore, that which she said, 'She is more righteous than I' — all the more so she should have been liable. And he [Ramban] explained: it was because he [Judah] was a ruler and governor in the land, and a daughter-in-law who commits harlotry against him is not judged by the law of other women who commit harlotry, but like one who insults the monarchy, and therefore he said, 'Bring her forth and let her be burnt,' for they came before him to do to her whatever he would command, and he sentenced her to death on account of the prerogative of the monarchy. And according to the peshat approach, the law was: 'who commits harlotry under her husband' — that they hand her over to her husband and he judges her to life or death as he wishes, and Tamar was designated for Shelah his son, and she was to them like a married woman in their customs. And R. Judah the Pious interpreted: Judah did not sentence her to burning, but rather that they should burn a mark between her eyes as a sign that she is a zona, and when Judah knew that it was from him and she was not a zona, they did nothing to her. They further question what Judah said, 'Bring her forth and let her be burnt' — but was not Judah a king, and a king neither judges nor is judged? And furthermore, in capital cases they begin from the side. And furthermore, how could he judge his own daughter-in-law? And they resolve: Isaac and Jacob and Judah judged her, and they began with Judah from the side. And in the Midrash: that Shem himself judged her — but this is impossible, for when you examine the reckoning of generation after generation you will find that he was already dead. And that which they said in Avodah Zarah, 'An unmarried man who has relations with an unmarried woman — the court of Shem decreed' — also because of the incident of Tamar, not that he [Shem] was still alive, but every court after him was called by his name."], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {1 "The phrase 'שאר נשים הזונות' is rendered as 'other women who commit harlotry.' The phrase 'באו לפניו לעשות לה את כל אשר יצוה' echoes the language of Esther; the translation preserves Judah's judicial authority without expanding on the intertextual echo."}, :source-witnesses [["Kitzur Baal HaTurim" "http://sefaria.org"] ["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:18:09.402883159Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}